If the server returns a successful HTTP response but the body is `null` or a non-object JSON value (e.g., a string or number), `await res.json()` will succeed but return a value that causes downstream `TypeError`s when accessing properties like `_err` or `banks` (e.g., `version._err` or `banksResp._err`). Guarding against non-object or `null` JSON responses ensures robust error handling.

```javascript
    if (asText) return await res.text();
    const json = await res.json();
