[Medium] YAML file detection uses substring match and misses YAML::BadFile, causing misparse and crashes

`findYamls` treats any argument that merely *contains* `.yaml`/`.yml` as an input file, and `setOptionsValuesFromYamlFile` only catches parse errors, so a missing/mistyped YAML path terminates the process uncaught.

**Affected files**
- `gemc/goptions/goptions.cc:285-295` (`findYamls`)
- `gemc/goptions/goptions.cc:308-319` (`setOptionsValuesFromYamlFile`)
- `gemc/goptions/goptions.cc:122` (file tokens skipped from option parsing)

**Details**

Defect 1 — substring match. `findYamls` classifies a token as a YAML input file when `.yaml`/`.yml` appears *anywhere*:

```cpp
string arg = argv[i];
size_t pos = arg.find(".yaml");
if (pos != string::npos) yaml_files.push_back(arg);
pos = arg.find(".yml");
if (pos != string::npos) yaml_files.push_back(arg);
```

An option value such as `-output_prefix=run.yaml.bak` or `-foo=a.ymlx` is misclassified as an input file. Worse, at line 122 these same tokens are then *skipped* from normal option parsing (`if (find(yaml_files...) != end) continue;`), so the option is silently dropped rather than applied. (A `.yaml` substring that is not a leading-`-` option also passes straight to `YAML::LoadFile`.)

Defect 2 — uncaught `YAML::BadFile`. The loader only catches `ParserException`:

```cpp
try {
    config = YAML::LoadFile(yaml);
}
catch (YAML::ParserException& e) {
    ...
    exit(EC__YAML_PARSING_ERROR);
}
```

`YAML::LoadFile` throws `YAML::BadFile` (a sibling of `ParserException`, both derive from `YAML::Exception`) when the path does not e

[Medium] CSV row newline only written inside the double-observables loop, dropped when a hit has no doubles

one-line summary
The CSV row terminator (`"\n"`) is emitted only on the final iteration of the double-valued observables loop; a hit with zero double observables never enters the loop body, so its row gets no newline and merges with the next row.

**Affected files**: `gemc/gstreamer/factories/CSV/event/publishDigitized.cc:54-58`, `gemc/gstreamer/factories/CSV/event/publishTrueInfo.cc:55-59`, `gemc/gstreamer/factories/CSV/run/publishDigitized.cc:55-59`

**Details**: In all three files the per-hit row is written as int observables (each followed by `", "`) then double observables, with the newline buried in the `else` branch of the double loop:

```cpp
// event/publishDigitized.cc
size_t total = dmap.size();
size_t i     = 0;
ofile_digitized << event_number << ", " << ... << detectorName << ", ";
for (const auto& [variableName, value] : imap) { ofile_digitized << value << ", "; }
for (const auto& [variableName, value] : dmap) {
    ofile_digitized << value;
    if (++i < total) ofile_digitized << ", ";
    else ofile_digitized << "\n";   // <-- only reached if dmap is non-empty
}
```

When `dmap.empty()` (`total == 0`), the loop body never executes, so neither the `", "`/`"\n"` separators run. The row ends with a trailing `", "` from the int loop and **no newline**, so the next hit's row is concatenated onto the same line. The same pattern (newline inside the `dmap`/`double` loop) appears in `publishTrueInfo.cc:55-59` and `run/publishDigitized.cc:55-59`.

